# BASE destination only — the chunk backend on Unraid (`kamal deploy`, no -d).
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE IS IGNORED UNDER `-d`. Kamal reads [.kamal/secrets-common, .kamal/secrets.<dest>],
# so adding a secret here does nothing for s3/share/ovh — it must go in that destination's own
# file. Shared keys (RAILS_MASTER_KEY, KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD) live in .kamal/secrets-common.
#
# Safe for git: references and commands only, never a raw credential.

# SeaweedFS chunk-store secret on the Unraid box, identity `seaweednew`
# (CHUNK_ACCESS_KEY_ID 2ER1TJXNXU15ECJ3YRQK, in config/deploy.yml in clear — an access key ID is
# an identifier, not a credential). Keep this reference and that ID together: they are one pair.
# ⚠️ The older `seaweedfs` 1Password item is RETIRED (it belonged to the dead .7 box) — signing
# with it fails InvalidAccessKeyId. `seaweednew` is the live one.
#
# The OVH leg reuses this VARIABLE NAME for a DIFFERENT credential (Cloudflare R2) — see
# .kamal/secrets.ovh. That is exactly why this reference is per-destination rather than shared:
# one name, two backends, and secrets-common cannot tell them apart.
CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=$(bin/secret CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY 'op://tinkerex/seaweednew/secret key')

# SYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN — required here NOT because the chunk backend's own container ever reads it (it
# doesn't; FileLayer::ChunkRemote::Provisioning is the only reader, and this destination runs plain
# FileLayer::Chunk), but because the `sync` accessory (config/deploy.yml, Arc D Task 2) declares
# SYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN as ITS OWN secret, and `accessories:` is never overridden by any destination
# overlay — so this destination's MERGED config names the key as required, same as every other
# destination (verified against Kamal::Configuration.raw_config for all five, not assumed). Deliberately
# NOT in .kamal/secrets-common (§14g): that file's `$(…)` lines run eagerly for every destination that
# reads it, and SYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN genuinely has a per-destination story that SECRET_KEY_BASE (which IS
# in secrets-common — see that file's own comment) does not: only the `remote` destination's own maker
# app additionally SENDS this credential (over HTTP, to the sync server's admin door). Repeating the
# same reference across every per-destination file keeps that distinction visible rather than blurring
# it into "just another common key". Fullest comment lives in .kamal/secrets.remote, which is the one
# copy that is functionally exercised beyond satisfying this file's own hygiene guard.
SYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN=$(bin/secret SYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN 'op://tinkerex/tinkerex-sync-admin-token/password')
